WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:34.000
Our story begins in 1982. CNN is only a few years old. Newspaper circulation isn't an all-time high, and every morning, around a million children take to their paper routes to deliver the morning news. On September 5, 1982, one of those children was a boy named Johnny Gosh, who slipped out of his house in West Des Moines, Iowa, before the sun came up, while his mom and dad were still asleep in their bed. We were awakened by the phone ringing.
我们的故事始于 1982 年。CNN 当时才成立几年。报纸发行量并未达到历史最高水平，每天早晨，大约有上百万名儿童开始他们的送报路线，去分发晨间新闻。1982 年 9 月 5 日，这些孩子中有一个名叫约翰尼·戈什（Johnny Gosh）的男孩，他在太阳升起之前溜出了位于爱荷华州西得梅因的家，当时他的父母还在床上熟睡。我们被一阵电话铃声吵醒。

2
00:00:34.000 --> 00:00:58.000
That's Noring Gosh, Johnny's mother. It was the neighbor saying they did not receive their newspapers. This wasn't like Johnny. He'd never missed a delivery. His dad got up and said, I'll go help him finish the route. He's probably just running late. So he went up the street, and that's where he saw Johnny's wagon sitting. Johnny's red wagon was sitting at the corner.
那是诺琳·戈什（Noring Gosh），约翰尼的母亲。是邻居打来的，说他们没有收到报纸。这可不像约翰尼会做的事。他从未漏掉过一次投递。他的爸爸起床说：“我去帮他送完剩下的。他大概只是迟到了。”于是他沿着街道走去，就在那里他看到了约翰尼的手推车。约翰尼的红色手推车正停在街角。

3
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:37.000
It was still full of newspapers. So he rushed back and told me Johnny was gone and to call the police. Johnny Gosh was 12 when he disappeared. He had blue eyes and dirty blonde hair with a small gap between his front teeth. And his face would be on the very first milk carton to ever feature a missing child. I have a distinct memory of being a kid and just staring at the milk carton with this blurry face on it. That's reporter Annie Brown. Which is weird because I definitely wasn't alive to see one.
车里还装满了报纸。于是他赶忙跑回来告诉我，约翰尼不见了，让我报警。约翰尼·戈什失踪时只有 12 岁。他有着蓝色的眼睛和一头深金色头发，门牙之间有一条小缝。他的脸将成为历史上第一个印有失踪儿童形象的牛奶盒封面。我对作为一个孩子时，盯着牛奶盒上那张模糊面孔的经历有着清晰的记忆。那是记者安妮·布朗（Annie Brown）。这很奇怪，因为我绝对没有经历过那个时代。

4
00:01:37.000 --> 00:02:35.000
The Mill Carton campaign only lasted a few years in the 80s, but it left an indelible mark on our culture. It introduced a kind of mythology around child safety that lingers to this day. And it changed what exactly Americans are afraid of when we fear for our children. From 99% Invisible and BBC Studios, this is a history of the United States and 100 Objects. I'm Roman Mars. Today, the missing children milk carton. The morning that Johnny disappeared, Noreen said it took 45 minutes for the police to arrive. So she called around to the other boys who delivered papers that morning.
牛奶盒运动在 20 世纪 80 年代只持续了几年，但它在我们的文化中留下了不可磨灭的印记。它围绕儿童安全引入了一种延续至今的神话。它改变了美国人在为孩子担心时，所恐惧的事物究竟是什么。本节目由 99% Invisible 与 BBC Studios 联合制作，为您带来《美国简史与 100 件物品》。我是罗曼·马尔斯（Roman Mars）。今天的主题是：失踪儿童牛奶盒。约翰尼失踪的那个早晨，诺琳说警察花了 45 分钟才赶到。于是她给那天早上送报的其他男孩打了电话。

5
00:02:35.000 --> 00:03:09.000
A few of them said they saw her son talking to a man in a blue car, but the police were skeptical. The cop looked at me and said, well, has your son ever run away before? And I said, well, no, he's never run away ever, nor did he run away today. This was Iowa. This kind of thing wasn't supposed to happen to kids like Johnny in places like Iowa. We'd never had a crime like this before. This was before Amber Alerts, before those text messages that you get when a kid goes missing. There wasn't even a category for missing children.
他们中有几个人说，看到她的儿子和一个开着蓝色汽车的男人说话，但警察对此持怀疑态度。警察看着我说：“好吧，你儿子以前离家出走过吗？”我说：“没有，他从来没有离家出走过，今天也没有。”这里是爱荷华州。这种事情本不应该发生在像约翰尼这样的人身上，在爱荷华州这样的小地方。我们以前从未发生过这样的罪案。这是安珀警报（Amber Alerts）出现之前，也是你在孩子失踪时收到的那些短信出现之前。当时甚至还没有“失踪儿童”这一分类。

6
00:03:09.000 --> 00:03:52.000
kids were put in the same group as missing adults, and an adult had to be gone for three days before they were considered missing. So without the police's help, Johnny's parents tried everything they could to find him. They hired private investigators and coordinated search parties. Meanwhile, as they searched, they also wrote Iowa state legislation that would differentiate missing children from missing adults. I wrote the John of Gosh law at my kitchen table. But after all of this, Noreen's son was still gone. And then two years later, in 1984, another little boy disappeared while delivering newspapers. He lived in a neighborhood not far from Johnny's.
孩子们被归入与失踪成年人相同的组别，而成年人必须失踪三天才能被视为失踪人员。因此，在没有警方帮助的情况下，约翰尼的父母尝试了他们能做到的一切来寻找他。他们雇用了私家侦探并协调搜救队。与此同时，在搜寻的过程中，他们还起草了爱荷华州立法，旨在将失踪儿童与失踪成年人区分开来。我在厨房的桌子上写下了《约翰尼·戈什法》。但经历了这一切之后，诺琳的儿子依然不见踪影。两年后的 1984 年，又有一个小男孩在送报时失踪了。他住在离约翰尼家不远的一个社区里。

7
00:03:52.000 --> 00:04:36.000
Eugene Martin was last seen delivering newspapers between 5.30 and 6 that morning. His paper bag with newspapers still inside was found just outside of Des Moines. This second paper boy had a relative who worked at a local dairy. The Anderson and Erickson Derry. That relative and the dairy's owner came up with an idea to help find these boys. The Anderson-Eriks & Derry contacted each family, and they wanted to do something helpful in the investigations. And within a few short weeks, thousands of milk cartons were rolled out of trucks and into grocery stores all over Des Moines. As clerks stocked their shelves, the faces of Johnny Gosh and Eugene Martin, smile.
尤金·马丁（Eugene Martin）最后一次被人看到是在那天早上 5 点半到 6 点之间送报。装着报纸的纸袋依然留在得梅因郊外。第二个送报男孩有一个在当地乳制品厂工作的亲戚。安德森-埃里克森乳业（The Anderson and Erickson Dairy）。这位亲戚和乳品厂老板想出了一个帮助寻找这些男孩的主意。安德森-埃里克森乳业联系了每个家庭，他们希望在调查中做一些有帮助的事情。在短短几周内，成千上万的牛奶盒从卡车上卸下，进入了得梅因各地的杂货店。当店员们将它们摆上货架时，约翰尼·戈什和尤金·马丁的微笑……

8
00:04:36.000 --> 00:05:01.000
The campaign started at one local dairy, but over the next few weeks, it spread to other Midwestern towns. And in Illinois, dairy saw it and said, why don't we try that? And from there, it went national. Nearly everybody drinks milk. Milk also turns over quickly, so we could put a lot of different kids' photos on and pull children's off that needed to come off. That's Barbara Huggett.
这场运动始于一家当地的乳制品厂，但在接下来的几周里，它蔓延到了中西部的其他城镇。伊利诺伊州的一家乳制品厂看到后说：“我们为什么不也试试呢？”从那以后，它走向了全国。几乎每个人都喝牛奶。牛奶的流通速度也很快，所以我们可以把许多不同孩子的照片印上去，并把那些需要撤下的孩子的照片换下来。那是芭芭拉·哈吉特（Barbara Huggett）。

9
00:05:01.000 --> 00:05:46.000
She was working as an artist at the National Child Safety Council in Michigan when the milk carton campaign took off. Our founder, apparently he'd seen Johnny Gosh and Eugene Martin on the Anderson-Erickson dairy milk cartons, but he felt that the program should be nationwide. A nationwide milk carton campaign would alert families in California to look for a child that went missing in Iowa. The national campaign brought together local groups looking for missing children, and together they came up with an initial list of 77 kids from all over the country. We used cases that were believed to be what they termed in those days dangerous stranger abductions. In other words, kids that weren't taken by someone they knew.
当牛奶盒运动兴起时，她正在密歇根州的全国儿童安全委员会（National Child Safety Council）担任美工。我们的创始人显然在安德森-埃里克森乳业的牛奶盒上看到了约翰尼·戈什和尤金·马丁，但他觉得这个项目应该在全国范围内推开。全国性的牛奶盒运动将提醒加利福尼亚州的家庭寻找在爱荷华州失踪的孩子。全国运动将寻找失踪儿童的当地组织聚集在一起，他们共同列出了来自全国各地的 77 名儿童的初始名单。我们采用了那些被认为是他们那个时代所谓的“危险陌生人绑架”的案件。换句话说，就是没有被他们认识的人带走的孩子。

10
00:05:46.000 --> 00:06:27.000
Barb's team didn't have computers, so they cut and arranged all the layouts by hand. We designed a layout that was just, you know, rather simple. In black block letters, the word missing was printed across the top of each waxy paper panel. photos of two children were laid side by side in black and white. Under the photos where there are dates of birth, height, weight, and where they were last seen. They also printed the pictures on school milk cartons, but only one child could fit on those. The National Child Safety Council reached out to dairies all over the country, asking if they'd be part of the campaign. Within months, 700 independent dairies had signed on.
芭芭拉的团队没有电脑，所以他们手工裁剪和排版了所有设计。我们设计了一个相当简单的版式。在黑色粗体字母中，“失踪”（missing）这个词印在每个蜡纸版块的顶部。两个孩子的照片黑白相间地并排摆放。照片下方是他们的出生日期、身高、体重以及最后一次被看到的地方。他们还将照片印在学校的牛奶盒上，但这些盒子上只能容纳一个孩子。全国儿童安全委员会联系了全国各地的乳制品厂，询问他们是否愿意参与这项运动。几个月内，就有 700 家独立乳制品厂签约加入。

11
00:06:27.000 --> 00:07:11.000
Boy, I wish I knew how many cartons had circulated. I imagine it's way into the billions when you have 700, 750 dairies printing day in and day out for years. Some sources have estimated that as many as 5 billion milk cartons were printed with the faces of missing children. Five billion milk cartons telling every American family this could happen to your kid. It's every parent's worst nightmare. As I said, we had not had cases of this going on in our community. Johnny's was the first really big case to hit. Johnny's may have been the first big case to hit
天哪，我真希望我知道发行了多少个牛奶盒。我想当你有 700、750 家乳品厂日复一日、年复一年地印刷时，数量已经达到了几十亿。一些消息来源估计，有多达 50亿个牛奶盒印上了失踪儿童的面孔。五十亿个牛奶盒在告诉每一个美国家庭，这可能会发生在你的孩子身上。这是每个父母最大的噩梦。正如我所说，我们的社区以前没有发生过这样的案件。约翰尼的是第一起真正轰动的大案。约翰尼的案子可能是第一起轰动的案件……

12
00:07:11.000 --> 00:07:42.000
and the first to spark the National Milk Carton campaign, but there had been previous kidnappings of other children in other places that hadn't sparked the same outrage. It's interesting. In Atlanta, there were two young boys who also went missing at around the same time in July of 1979. This is historian Paul Renfro. And this didn't really initiate the same sort of panic, the same sort of concern that you saw in Iowa
也是第一起引发全国牛奶盒运动的案件，但在此之前，其他地方也发生过其他儿童绑架案，却没有引发同等的愤怒。这很有趣。在亚特兰大，也有两个小男孩在 1979 年 7 月左右的大致同一时间失踪。这是历史学家保罗·伦弗罗（Paul Renfro）。但这并没有真正引发在爱荷华州因两名送报男孩失踪而引发的那种恐慌和关注。在亚特兰大失踪的不仅仅是两个男孩。而是一整串人。看到世界做出的反应如此截然不同，真是令人震惊。首先，一个 14 岁的男孩名叫特迪·史密斯（Teddy Smith）失踪了。四天后，13 岁的阿尔弗雷德·埃文斯（Alfred Evans）失踪。几个月后，另一个 14 岁的男孩在为妈妈去银行的路上消失了，接着是一个为邻居跑腿的九岁男孩。全国主流媒体基本保持沉默。

13
00:07:42.000 --> 00:08:14.000
with the disappearances of two paper boys. It wasn't just two boys in Atlanta who disappeared. It was a whole slew of them. And it's striking to see just how differently the world responded. First, a 14th. year old boy named Teddy Smith went missing. Four days later, 13-year-old Alfred Evans disappears. A few months after that, another 14-year-old vanishes on the way to the bank for his mom, then a nine-year-old running an errand for a neighbor. The national media was largely quiet.
没有创新性的运动来寻找他们，但这种事一次又一次地发生。事实上，在两年的时间里，有 29 名儿童和年轻人 在亚特兰大失踪。一个明显的区别是，这些孩子全部都是黑人。所有这些年轻人都是非裔美国人，围绕它的舆论，除了受害者的家属之外，并没有来自公众的真正的呐喊。为什么亚特兰大的绑架事件没有引发类似运动？我认为这与我们对童年和童年纯真的理解有关。

14
00:08:14.000 --> 00:08:54.000
There was no innovative campaign to find them, but it happened over and over again. In fact, over the course of two years, 29 kids and young people disappeared in Atlanta. One clear difference was that all of these children were black. All of these youths were African American, and the discourse around it, there was no real kind of cry from the public apart from the victim's families. And why didn't the Atlanta kidnappings lead to like a similar campaign? I think it has to do with our understandings of childhood and childhood innocence.
我认为这些事情总是以非常特定的大众心理被种族化。因此，那些如此轻易地贴在爱荷华州的约翰尼·戈什和尤金·韦德·马丁以及其他失踪或遇害的白人儿童身上的纯真描述，并没有被应用到在亚特兰大被绑架和杀害的年轻人身上。他们经常被描述为精明、街头混混。因此，在某种程度上，围绕他们失踪和死亡的一些言论，反而起到了使他们的失踪和死亡合理化的作用。亚特兰大被绑架的许多儿童在失踪后的几周和几个月内被发现已经死亡。但有些人依然下落不明。然而，他们中没有一个人出现在全国儿童安全委员会的牛奶盒运动中。即使对于那些最终登上牛奶盒的孩子来说，这项运动也基本算不上成功。

15
00:08:54.000 --> 00:09:48.000
I think those things are always racialized in very particular ways. And so the descriptors of innocence that were so readily attached to Johnny Gosh and Eugene Wade Martin in Iowa and other missing or slain white kids, those descriptors were not applied to the youngsters who were kidnapped and killed in Atlanta. Oftentimes they were described as streetwise, as street hustlers. And so in a way, some of the discourse surrounding their disappearances and deaths serve to legitimate their disappearances and deaths. Many of the children kidnapped in Atlanta were found dead in the weeks and months after they disappeared. But some remained missing. And yet none of them appeared in the National Child Safety Council's Milk Carton campaign. And even for the kids who did end up on milk cartons, the campaign was largely unsuccessful.
在全国儿童安全委员会运动中亮相的 200 个孩子中，只有两个被活着找到了。芭芭拉仍然记得他们接到电话的那天，得知一个名叫小鲍比·史密斯（Bobby Smith Jr.）的男孩被找到了。他们打电话说他被找到了的那天，我们通过对讲机的扬声器让所有人都知道了。那是一种欣喜若狂的感觉，你想让大楼里每一个邮寄过、打包过、发货过的人都知道。你知道，你只是想告诉每一个摸过它的人，这一切都是值得的。你能听到整栋大楼里欢呼雀跃。太好了，我们找到了一个孩子。这真是太棒了，你知道的。但小鲍比·史密斯并不是因为牛奶盒而被找到的。鲍比是巧合被找到的。他绑架者的汽车卷入了一场事故。当警察来寻找车主时，他们找到了鲍比。

16
00:09:48.000 --> 00:10:15.000
Of the 200 kids who were featured in the National Child Safety Council's campaign, only two were ever found alive. Barbara still remembers the day they got the call that a boy named Bobby Smith Jr. was found. The day they called and said that he had been found, we got on the loudspeaker on an intercom and let everyone know. And it was just a sense of elation, and you wanted to let everyone in the building that had mailed it,
但全国运动和较小的本地运动中大多数特色儿童从未被找到。不幸的是，来自牛奶盒的线索并没有揭示约翰尼的下落。那是诺琳·戈什。她的儿子约翰尼永远没有被找到。但她和芭芭拉·哈吉特都坚信这场运动产生了积极的影响。它所做的是提高了人们的意识，让人们开始寻找。他们看到了一个看起来处于痛苦中的孩子。也许你会质疑那是不是亲生父母。

17
00:10:15.000 --> 00:10:43.000
boxed it, shipped it. You know, you just wanted to tell everyone who had touched it that was worthwhile. You could hear the cheer throughout the whole building. Yay, we found a child. It was wonderful, you know. But Bobby Smith wasn't found because of the milk carton. Bobby was found by coincidence. His kidnapper's car had been involved in an accident. And when the police came looking for the owner of the car, they found Bobby.
大多数情况下，牛奶盒只是让人们开始思考绑架问题。我和相当多的成年人交流过，他们还记得他们的妈妈把牛奶盒放在厨房桌子上说：“现在，我们要重新过一遍规则，”他们会以牛奶盒上的男孩为例。然后将那个孩子与他们自己的生活建立联系。换句话说，牛奶盒上的面孔把所有人都吓坏了。

18
00:10:43.000 --> 00:11:19.000
But most of the kids featured in the national campaign and in the smaller, local ones were never found. Unfortunately, the leads from the milk cartons did not reveal where Johnny's location was. That's Noreen Gosh again. Her son, Johnny, was never found. But she and Barbara Huggett are both adamant that the campaign had a positive impact. What it did was promote more awareness so that people were looking. They saw a child that looked like they were in distress. Maybe you questioned whether or not those were the parents.
这最终导致了运动的没落。几乎在牛奶盒运动开始的同时，它们也开始走向尾声。乳业开始从侧面板上撤下失踪儿童的照片。当然，原因之一是行业转向了塑料包装。但乳品厂也开始感到厌烦。

19
00:11:19.000 --> 00:11:49.000
Mostly, the milk cartons just got people thinking about kidnapping. I have talked to quite a number of adults, and they could remember their mom putting the milk carton on the kitchen table and saying, now, we're going to go over the rules again, and they would use the example of the boys on the milk carton. And then make the correlation between that child and their child's life. In other words, the faces on the milk cartons scared the hell out of everyone.
这种压抑的信息。他们把它撤下来，是因为有人抱怨他们认为这可能会在早餐桌上吓到儿童。著名的儿科医生本杰明·斯波克（Benjamin Spock）博士谴责了牛奶盒运动。他说，这是在对人进行心理创伤。

20
00:11:49.000 --> 00:12:10.000
Which ultimately led to the campaign's downfall. Almost as quickly as they began, the milk carton campaigns started to wind down. Darius began to pull the missing kids' pictures from their side panels. One reason, of course, with the cartons is that the industry shifted to plastic. But Darius also started to get tired
孩子们不应该在他们的幸运符麦片上看到这些。他们看到这些失踪的孩子仿佛在盯着他们看。那个牛奶盒几乎迫使他们进行那场对话。如果你看到陌生人或陌生人搭讪该怎么办。那是历史学家保罗·伦弗罗。保罗写了一本关于牛奶盒儿童的书，以及当时席卷社会的巨大恐慌。陌生人危险。保罗研究了当时在新闻、广播和政治家中谈论儿童安全的方式。

21
00:12:10.000 --> 00:12:25.000
of the depressing message. They took it away because there were people that were complaining that they thought it might scare children at the breakfast table. The famous pediatrician, Dr. Benjamin Spock, condemned the milk carton campaign. It was traumatizing, he said.
人们四处散布那些简直是天文数字的数字。人们说美国有 180 万失踪儿童。而“失踪”与“被陌生人绑架”紧密相连。但这根本不是真的。每年大约有 350,000 名儿童被报失踪给当局。大约每天 1,000 人。但联邦调查局（FBI）最近的报告发现，实际被陌生人带走的儿童数量仅占其中的 0.1%。

